Description: fix the h264e poll cfg allocation size and its single cfg indexing
 The four h264e vepu5xx HALs size their MppDevPollCfg allocation with
 sizeof(p->poll_cfgs), which is the size of the pointer (8) rather than the
 size of the struct it points at (16). The result is 40 bytes per config while
 mpp_service_cmd_poll() declares sizeof(*cfg) + count_max * 4 == 48 bytes to
 the kernel, so the kernel is licensed to write eight slice records into a
 buffer that has room for six.
 .
 The vepu580 and vepu510 HALs allocate one config per task and index them by
 poll_cfg_size, so the stride is 40 while each config spans 48: task N's
 slice_info[6] and slice_info[7] alias task N+1's poll_type and poll_ret. That
 is harmless only while both fields are unused. The trailing overrun past the
 end of the allocation is masked today by the allocator's 32-byte rounding.
 .
 Use sizeof(MppDevPollCfg). The four h265e HALs multiply their size by two and
 are already large enough, so they are left alone.
 .
 vepu511a additionally indexed a single config allocation. It allocates one
 MppDevPollCfg and one HalVepu511aRegSet, and uses regs_set unindexed
 everywhere else, but its wait path derived the poll cfg pointer as
 poll_cfgs + task->flags.reg_idx * poll_cfg_size, copied from the multi task
 vepu580 HAL. reg_idx is always zero for a single task HAL so this never fires
 in practice, but it is an out of bounds read and write for any non-zero
 reg_idx, and correcting poll_cfg_size above would have widened that window
 from 40 to 48 bytes rather than closing it. Use the single config directly,
 as hal_h264e_vepu511.c already does. MAX_TASK_CNT is dead in that file.
